The '''History of Sarta (1747–1811)''' covers the period from the consolidation of [[Maria I of Sarta]]'s rule after the earlier civil wars to the end of the [[Sartan Civil War]] in 1811. It was marked by the persistence of a constitutional monarchy in formal terms, the rise of a more oligarchic political order, and the gradual realignment of Sartan society around noble estates, baronial power, and Volisanian influence. The kingdom remained internally divided throughout the period, and by its end it had been reduced to a subordinate position under Volisania.

== Maria I and the postwar settlement ==

After the fighting of the earlier period, the royal parliamentary court remained in place and [[Maria I of Sarta]] emerged as the dominant figure in Sartan politics. The new order reflected the influence of liberalismo in other Illypnian states, but in practice it was shaped more by elite bargaining than by broader reform. The crown continued to govern through the parliament rather than against it, yet the system remained highly restrictive and deeply unequal.

Maria I depended on the support of the baron class, which had become central to the functioning of the state. Her house needed elite backing to preserve the monarchy, while the barons expected influence in return. The result was a constitutional monarchy in form but an oligarchy in practice. Political authority was concentrated among nobles, court factions, and major landholders, and the wealth of the kingdom continued to circulate mainly among those groups.

The capital remained at [[Mariapolis]] on [[Lake Divisora]], which had become the main political centre by the end of the previous era. The court was still formally royal and parliamentary, but it increasingly functioned as a gathering point for factional negotiation rather than as a vehicle for broad reform.

== Social realignment ==

The decades after 1747 saw a wider realignment of Sartan society. Royal lands and other properties were sold to nobles, wealthy families, and estate holders in order to stabilise royal finances and maintain political loyalty. Some of the wealthiest Sartans moved to [[Guajicutea]] or to Volisanian-controlled Sartan territory, where coastal trade and security remained stronger. Others remained in the kingdom and expanded their inland holdings.

This redistribution strengthened the baronial class, but it did not spread wealth to the wider population. Most rural people remained tied to feudal farms, and the state did little to alter that structure. Rights remained limited, movement was restricted in many districts, and the relationship between government and countryside became increasingly coercive.

The former coastal wealth of Sarta remained a source of resentment. Much of the richest territory along the coast was still under Volisanian control, and many elite families hoped that those lands would eventually be recovered. At the same time, others accepted the new balance of power and used it to build their own fortunes. The result was a society divided between inland royalists, coastal interests, Volisanian sympathisers, and local landed elites.

== Court politics and unrest ==

Maria I retained the parliamentary settlement, but her government became more rigid over time. The royal court remained influential, yet it did not open the political system in any meaningful way. Instead, the state became more absolutist in practice, relying on patronage, legal controls, and the cooperation of powerful families.

Peasant revolts flared repeatedly in the countryside. The government responded with tighter restrictions on travel, assembly, and local movement, especially in districts where unrest was strongest. These measures reduced the scale of open rebellion, but they also deepened hostility toward the crown and the barons.

The elite itself was not unified. Some nobles supported a stronger royal court, others preferred a looser parliamentary balance, and some remained quietly attached to Volisania because of trade or regional ties. The political order therefore remained unstable even in periods of relative peace.

== Volisanian influence ==

Volisanian influence increased gradually through the period. The loss of the coast in the previous war had left Sarta vulnerable, and Volisania retained leverage through trade, territory, and local allies. The richest parts of the former Sartan coast remained outside Sartan control, which kept the question of recovery alive in political debate.

For many inland Sartans, Volisania represented both a threat and a model of power. For others, it was a practical partner that could stabilise trade and reinforce the rule of a compliant court. That tension shaped the whole period. Maria I's court never fully escaped suspicion that it was too close to Volisania, while Volisania itself treated Sarta as a useful but subordinate neighbour.

== The Second Sartan Civil War ==

By the end of the eighteenth century, the accumulated pressures of elite rivalry, rural unrest, and foreign influence produced another civil war. The [[Second Sartan Civil War]] of 1799–1811 broke out in a kingdom already weakened by decades of inequality and internal division. The conflict was one of the worst in Sartan history, killing roughly 8.5% of the population.

The war reflected both social and dynastic conflict. Some factions wanted a stronger royal settlement, others backed the parliamentary elite, and others hoped to settle the struggle with outside support. Volisania intervened directly and used the opportunity to impose a new political order. At the end of the war, Volisania placed [[Alfonso II of Sarta]] on the throne, giving the kingdom a Volisanian-backed king and formalising Sarta's subordination.

The intervention ended the civil war, but it did not restore Sartan independence. Instead, it confirmed that the kingdom's sovereignty had been narrowed by decades of internal division and foreign pressure. By 1811, the population was estimated at about 4.2 million; the first official census in 1815 would later give the kingdom its first systematic population baseline.

== Aftermath ==

By 1811, Sarta had become a more stratified and dependent state than it had been in 1747. The royal parliamentary court survived, but only as part of a political order shaped by estate wealth, baronial power, rural restraint, and Volisanian intervention. The crown remained in place, yet the kingdom was no longer acting as an independent regional power.

The period left behind a society realigned around noble holdings, inland power centres, and the political needs of a foreign-backed monarchy. It also set the stage for the next phase of Sartan history, in which the kingdom would remain under Volisanian influence and continue to struggle over the balance between royal authority, parliamentary institutions, and popular discontent.

